   "difficulty": 2,
   "stem_html": "<p>Solve</p><p style=\"text-align:center\">9<sup><span class=\"math\">x</span></sup> × 27<sup>1 − <span class=\"math\">x</span></sup> = <span class=\"fr\"><span>3<sup><span class=\"math\">x</span> + 4</sup></span><span>81</span></span></p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = −1",
    "B": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = <span class=\"fr\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span>",
    "C": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = 1",
    "D": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = 3",
    "E": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = <span class=\"fr\"><span>7</span><span>2</span></span>"
   },
   "answer": "B",
   "solution_html": "<p>Everything in base 3. Left: 3<sup>2<span class=\"math\">x</span></sup> × 3<sup>3 − 3<span class=\"math\">x</span></sup> = 3<sup>3 − <span class=\"math\">x</span></sup>. Right: 3<sup><span class=\"math\">x</span> + 4 − 4</sup> = 3<sup><span class=\"math\">x</span></sup>.</p><p>3 − <span class=\"math\">x</span> = <span class=\"math\">x</span>, so <span class=\"key\">x = 3/2</span>.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "A": "Comes from 3 − x = x + 4 (forgetting to subtract the 4 from the 81).",
    "C": "Comes from 2x + 3 − 3x = x with a sign slip giving 3 = 3x.",
    "D": "Comes from 3 − x = 0.",
    "E": "Comes from 3 − x = x − 4."
   },
   "detail": "Laws of indices"
  },
  {
   "n": 3,
   "topic": "Algebra & polynomials",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.3"
   ],
   "difficulty": 4,
   "stem_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">k</span> is a real constant. For how many values of <span class=\"math\">k</span> does the equation</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><span class=\"math\">kx</span><sup>2</sup> + (<span class=\"math\">k</span> + 1)<span class=\"math\">x</span> + 1 = 0</p><p>have <strong>exactly one</strong> real solution?</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "0",
    "B": "1",
    "C": "2",
    "D": "3",
    "E": "Infinitely many"
   },
   "answer": "C",
   "solution_html": "<p>If <span class=\"math\">k</span> ≠ 0 it is a quadratic with discriminant (<span class=\"math\">k</span> + 1)<sup>2</sup> − 4<span class=\"math\">k</span> = (<span class=\"math\">k</span> − 1)<sup>2</sup>. Exactly one (repeated) root when <span class=\"math\">k</span> = 1.</p><p>If <span class=\"math\">k</span> = 0 the equation is <span class=\"math\">x</span> + 1 = 0, which is linear and has exactly one solution.</p><p>So <span class=\"key\">2</span> values: <span class=\"math\">k</span> = 0 and <span class=\"math\">k</span> = 1.</p><p class=\"trap\">\"Exactly one real solution\" is not the same as \"repeated root\". The degenerate case k = 0 is the whole point.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "A": "Discriminant (k − 1)² is zero at k = 1.",
    "B": "k = 1 gives the repeated root, but k = 0 also gives one solution because the equation becomes linear.",
    "D": "There is no third value; (k − 1)² has one root.",
    "E": "(k − 1)² is positive for every k except 1, so the quadratic usually has two distinct roots."
   },
   "detail": "Roots of an equation with a parameter"
  },
  {
   "n": 4,
   "topic": "Algebra & polynomials",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.5"
   ],
   "difficulty": 2,
   "stem_html": "<p>The complete set of values of <span class=\"math\">x</span> satisfying both <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> + <span class=\"math\">x</span> − 6 ≥ 0 and <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> &lt; 4<span class=\"math\">x</span> is</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "2 ≤ <span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 4",
    "B": "2 &lt; <span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 4",
    "C": "0 &lt; <span class=\"math\">x</span> ≤ 2",
    "D": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> ≤ −3 or <span class=\"math\">x</span> ≥ 2",
    "E": "−3 ≤ <span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 0"
   },
   "answer": "A",
   "solution_html": "<p>(<span class=\"math\">x</span> + 3)(<span class=\"math\">x</span> − 2) ≥ 0 gives <span class=\"math\">x</span> ≤ −3 or <span class=\"math\">x</span> ≥ 2. <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> − 4<span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 0 gives 0 &lt; <span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 4.</p><p>Both: <span class=\"key\">2 ≤ x < 4</span>. The 2 is included because the first inequality is non-strict.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "B": "x = 2 satisfies both: 4 + 2 − 6 = 0 ≥ 0 and 4 < 8.",
    "C": "Uses the region between the roots for the first inequality.",
    "D": "First inequality only.",
    "E": "Divided x² < 4x by x without knowing its sign."
   },
   "detail": "Quadratic inequalities"
  },
  {
   "n": 5,
   "topic": "Algebra & polynomials",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.6"
   ],
   "difficulty": 2,
   "stem_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">p</span>(<span class=\"math\">x</span>) = <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>3</sup> − 2<span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> + <span class=\"math\">ax</span> + <span class=\"math\">b</span>. When <span class=\"math\">p</span>(<span class=\"math\">x</span>) is divided by (<span class=\"math\">x</span> − 1) the remainder is 4, and (<span class=\"math\">x</span> + 1) is a factor of <span class=\"math\">p</span>(<span class=\"math\">x</span>).</p><p>What is the remainder when <span class=\"math\">p</span>(<span class=\"math\">x</span>) is divided by (<span class=\"math\">x</span> − 2)?</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "0",
    "B": "4",
    "C": "5",
    "D": "6",
    "E": "8"
   },
   "answer": "D",
   "solution_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">p</span>(1) = 1 − 2 + <span class=\"math\">a</span> + <span class=\"math\">b</span> = 4, so <span class=\"math\">a</span> + <span class=\"math\">b</span> = 5. <span class=\"math\">p</span>(−1) = −1 − 2 − <span class=\"math\">a</span> + <span class=\"math\">b</span> = 0, so <span class=\"math\">b</span> − <span class=\"math\">a</span> = 3.</p><p><span class=\"math\">b</span> = 4, <span class=\"math\">a</span> = 1. <span class=\"math\">p</span>(2) = 8 − 8 + 2 + 4 = <span class=\"key\">6</span>.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "A": "Assumes (x − 2) is also a factor.",
    "B": "Copies the remainder for (x − 1).",
    "C": "a + b, not p(2).",
    "E": "Forgets the −2x² term."
   },
   "detail": "Factor and remainder theorems"
  },
  {
